# NestJS Architecture Rules

## Feature Modules

Every domain concept is a NestJS module. `AppModule` only imports feature modules and global infrastructure modules (database, config, auth).

```
AppModule
  ├── ConfigModule (global)
  ├── TypeOrmModule (global)
  ├── AuthModule
  ├── UsersModule
  ├── OrdersModule
  └── ProductsModule
```

If `AppModule` directly imports more than ~10 feature modules, introduce domain-level grouping modules (e.g., `CatalogModule` wrapping `ProductsModule` + `CategoriesModule`). But do not group prematurely.

## Controllers Are Thin

One service call per handler method. No business logic. No `if` statements beyond basic guard delegation.

```typescript
// CORRECT — thin controller
@Get(':id')
async findOne(@Param('id', ParseUUIDPipe) id: string): Promise<UserResponseDto> {
  return this.usersService.findOne(id);
}

// WRONG — business logic in controller
@Get(':id')
async findOne(@Param('id') id: string) {
  const user = await this.usersService.findOne(id);
  if (!user) throw new NotFoundException();   // belongs in service
  if (user.role === 'admin') { ... }          // business logic — belongs in service
  return user;
}
```

## DTOs for Every Input

Every POST/PUT/PATCH body gets a DTO class with `class-validator` decorators. `ValidationPipe` (configured globally with `whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true`) strips unknown fields and throws 400 on invalid input.

```typescript
export class CreateUserDto {
  @IsString()
  @IsNotEmpty()
  name: string;

  @IsEmail()
  email: string;

  @IsString()
  @MinLength(8)
  password: string;
}
```

Never accept `body: any` or `body: Record<string, unknown>` from a request.

## Response DTOs

Transform entities to response DTOs in the service layer. Never return raw TypeORM entities from controllers — they expose unintended relations, lazy-load columns, and sensitive fields.

```typescript
// Service returns a typed ResponseDto, not the entity
async findOne(id: string): Promise<UserResponseDto> {
  const user = await this.usersRepository.findOneOrFail(id);
  return plainToInstance(UserResponseDto, user, { excludeExtraneousValues: true });
}
```

Alternatively, use `@Exclude()` on entity fields + `ClassSerializerInterceptor` globally — but be explicit about what is excluded.

## Guards for Auth

Authentication and authorization live exclusively in guards.

```typescript
@Controller('users')
@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)          // applies to all routes in this controller
export class UsersController { ... }

@Get('admin-only')
@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard, RolesGuard)   // additional route-level guard
@Roles('admin')
async adminRoute() { ... }
```

Never decode or validate a JWT token inside a controller handler. Never pass `request.user` from the raw request without a guard having already validated it.

## Interceptors for Cross-Cutting Concerns

Register globally in `main.ts`:

```typescript
app.useGlobalInterceptors(new ClassSerializerInterceptor(app.get(Reflector)));
```

Custom interceptors for: logging, distributed tracing, response envelope wrapping, caching headers. Keep interceptors focused — one concern per interceptor.

## Pipes for Parameter Transformation

Use built-in pipes on route parameters — never cast manually:

```typescript
@Get(':id')
findOne(@Param('id', ParseUUIDPipe) id: string) { ... }

@Get('page/:page')
findPage(@Param('page', ParseIntPipe) page: number) { ... }
```

## Exception Filters

Domain errors surface through a custom exception filter registered globally:

```typescript
@Catch(DomainException)
export class DomainExceptionFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  catch(exception: DomainException, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    const ctx = host.switchToHttp();
    const response = ctx.getResponse<Response>();
    response.status(exception.httpStatus).json({ message: exception.message });
  }
}
```

Use `NotFoundException`, `BadRequestException`, `ForbiddenException` from `@nestjs/common` for standard HTTP errors. Never throw plain `Error` objects from services.
